  "abstract": "Communication tools for deaf people often work in one direction: they help hearing people understand sign language, but they do not reliably return information in sign language for deaf users. DuoSign was built to address this gap. It is a web-based system that takes English input and presents the output as animated American Sign Language (ASL), without requiring specialised hardware. DuoSign follows a three-stage workflow: it converts English text into ASL gloss, maps each gloss to sign motion data derived from real signing videos, and renders the result through a 3D signing avatar. The system also includes fallback strategies such as synonym matching and fingerspelling to reduce missing-word failures. Because the motion data is grounded in human signing video, the produced animations are generally more natural than purely notation-driven output. A key contribution of this work is portability. The same pipeline can be reused for other sign languages when video resources are available, including low-resource settings where large annotated corpora are limited. In this sense, DuoSign is both a working ASL generation tool and a practical baseline for communities that want to bootstrap their own sign-language accessibility systems.",
